To fix the provider mismatch without losing your work or data, follow these steps:

### 1. Reset Migration History Only
```bash:terminal
cd electgo
rm -rf prisma/migrations
```

### 2. Create New Migration
```bash:terminal
pnpm prisma migrate dev --name init --create-only
```

### 3. Manually Update Migration Lock
Edit the new migration file to match your SQLite provider:

```bash:terminal
# Open the newly created migration file
# (replace TIMESTAMP with your actual migration timestamp)
code prisma/migrations/TIMESTAMP_init/migration.sql
```

Add this line at the top:
```sql
-- provider: sqlite
```

### 4. Apply Migration
```bash:terminal
pnpm prisma migrate dev
```

### 5. Verify and Continue
```bash:terminal
pnpm prisma generate
pnpm prisma db seed
pnpm dev
```

### Why this works:
1. `--create-only` creates migration files without applying them
2. Manually adding `-- provider: sqlite` satisfies Prisma's lock check
3. The existing SQLite database (`dev.db`) remains untouched
4. Your data stays intact while fixing the migration history

### Alternative One-Liner:
```bash:terminal
cd electgo && rm -rf prisma/migrations && pnpm prisma migrate dev --name init --create-only && sed -i "1i -- provider: sqlite" prisma/migrations/*/migration.sql && pnpm prisma migrate dev && pnpm prisma generate && pnpm prisma db seed
```

This approach preserves your database while fixing the provider mismatch in the migration system. Your existing data in `dev.db` will remain safe and accessible.

